{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1788475199815,"sessionID":"ses_f9695abb0ffeRAswhwJBPpOv52","part":{"id":"prt_0696d7ff90010A2y64k3JneD7q","messageID":"msg_0696d7255001fLEvPQzuK8D8sU","sessionID":"ses_f9695abb0ffeRAswhwJBPpOv52","type":"reasoning","text":"Wait, there's a potential issue: `auth_test_result.headers` — if `auth_test_result` is a plain dict (like `{}` or a dict with data), `.headers` would raise AttributeError. In the sync test, `SingleTeamAuthorization(auth_test_result={})` passes `{}`, which is falsy, so `if not self.auth_test_result:` triggers the client call. But the `if self.auth_test_result:` check — a real SlackResponse is truthy? Let me check `SlackResponse.__bool__`... Actually SlackResponse defines `__contains__` and `get`... let me check if it has `__bool__`. Looking at slack_sdk's SlackResponse:\n\n```python\ndef __bool__(self) -> bool:\n    return self.data[\"ok\"] is True\n```\n\nHmm, I need to verify. But regardless — in the sync flow, when `auth_test_result` is `{}`, the middleware calls the client, so `.headers` access happens on a real SlackResponse. When it's a non-empty dict... that would break. But that matches upstream exactly, and `SlackResponse`/`AsyncSlackResponse` is the documented type.\n\nActually wait, let me double-check one thing: does `SlackResponse` have `headers` when created from the mock server? Yes — `_urllib_api_call` passes `headers=dict(response[\"headers\"])`.\n\nOne concern: with `http.client`, response headers from our mock server are case-preserved as sent: `x-oauth-scopes`. `dict(email.message.Message)` — hmm wait, in `_perform_urllib_http_request`, `resp[\"headers\"]` is the raw `email.message.Message` object (`return {\"status\": resp.code, \"headers\": resp.headers, \"body\": body}`), and then in `_urllib_api_call`: `headers=dict(response[\"headers\"])`.\n\n`dict()` on a Message: Message implements `keys()` and `__getitem__`, and `dict(mapping)` uses the `keys()` protocol. So `dict(message)` gives `{header_name: value}` with original casing. Good — `x-oauth-scopes` lowercase as sent by the server. Real Slack API also sends lowercase `x-oauth-scopes` (HTTP/2). Good.\n\nBut wait — for `Message`, `dict(message)` iterates keys... Actually let me verify Python's dict constructor: if the argument has a `keys` method, it's treated as a mapping: `dict(m)` = `{k: m[k] for k in m.keys()}`. Message has `keys()` and `__getitem__`. So yes.\n\nHmm, but one more subtlety: `Message.keys()` may return duplicate header names (e.g., multiple `set-cookie`). Fine for our purposes.\n\nAlso check: does `Message.__getitem__` lowercase the key lookup? Yes — `email.message.Message` lookups are case-insensitive, but `dict()` conversion is case-preserving, so `.get(\"x-oauth-scopes\")` requires exact lowercase key. Since we control the mock server (sends lowercase) and Slack sends lowercase, fine — matches upstream exactly.\n\nFor aiohttp AsyncSlackResponse: headers is a `CIMultiDict` — case-insensitive `.get()`. Fine.\n\nNow the second change: mock_web_api_server.py headers.","time":{"start":1788475179001,"end":1788475199790},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, there's a potential issue: `auth_test_result.headers` — if `auth_test_result` is a plain dict (like `{}` or a dict with data), `.headers` would raise AttributeError. In the sync test, `SingleTeamAuthorization(auth_test_result={})` passes `{}`, which is falsy, so `if not self.auth_test_result:` triggers the client call. But the `if self.auth_test_result:` check — a real SlackResponse is truthy? Let me check `SlackResponse.__bool__`... Actually SlackResponse defines `__contains__` and `get`... let me check if it has `__bool__`. Looking at slack_sdk's SlackResponse:\n\n```python\ndef __bool__(self) -> bool:\n    return self.data[\"ok\"] is True\n```\n\nHmm, I need to verify. But regardless — in the sync flow, when `auth_test_result` is `{}`, the middleware calls the client, so `.headers` access happens on a real SlackResponse. When it's a non-empty dict... that would break. But that matches upstream exactly, and `SlackResponse`/`AsyncSlackResponse` is the documented type.\n\nActually wait, let me double-check one thing: does `SlackResponse` have `headers` when created from the mock server? Yes — `_urllib_api_call` passes `headers=dict(response[\"headers\"])`.\n\nOne concern: with `http.client`, response headers from our mock server are case-preserved as sent: `x-oauth-scopes`. `dict(email.message.Message)` — hmm wait, in `_perform_urllib_http_request`, `resp[\"headers\"]` is the raw `email.message.Message` object (`return {\"status\": resp.code, \"headers\": resp.headers, \"body\": body}`), and then in `_urllib_api_call`: `headers=dict(response[\"headers\"])`.\n\n`dict()` on a Message: Message implements `keys()` and `__getitem__`, and `dict(mapping)` uses the `keys()` protocol. So `dict(message)` gives `{header_name: value}` with original casing. Good — `x-oauth-scopes` lowercase as sent by the server. Real Slack API also sends lowercase `x-oauth-scopes` (HTTP/2). Good.\n\nBut wait — for `Message`, `dict(message)` iterates keys... Actually let me verify Python's dict constructor: if the argument has a `keys` method, it's treated as a mapping: `dict(m)` = `{k: m[k] for k in m.keys()}`. Message has `keys()` and `__getitem__`. So yes.\n\nHmm, but one more subtlety: `Message.keys()` may return duplicate header names (e.g., multiple `set-cookie`). Fine for our purposes.\n\nAlso check: does `Message.__getitem__` lowercase the key lookup? Yes — `email.message.Message` lookups are case-insensitive, but `dict()` conversion is case-preserving, so `.get(\"x-oauth-scopes\")` requires exact lowercase key. Since we control the mock server (sends lowercase) and Slack sends lowercase, fine — matches upstream exactly.\n\nFor aiohttp AsyncSlackResponse: headers is a `CIMultiDict` — case-insensitive `.get()`. Fine.\n\nNow the second change: mock_web_api_server.py headers.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}}}
