{"type":"text","timestamp":1788475441440,"sessionID":"ses_f9695abb0ffeRAswhwJBPpOv52","part":{"id":"prt_069716b33001ZVU4DI63XLULG1","messageID":"msg_069715ef9001h6sQjE7JPFWidS","sessionID":"ses_f9695abb0ffeRAswhwJBPpOv52","type":"text","text":"Done. `SingleTeamAuthorization` now exposes OAuth scopes in `context.authorize_result`, matching `MultiTeamsAuthorization` behavior:\n\n**Core change** — slack_bolt/middleware/authorization/internals.py:71\n- `_to_authorize_result()` now reads the `x-oauth-scopes` header from the `auth.test` response and assigns it based on token type: `bot_scopes` for `xoxb-` tokens, `user_scopes` for user tokens. Since it's shared by both `SingleTeamAuthorization` (slack_bolt/middleware/authorization/single_team_authorization.py:69) and `AsyncSingleTeamAuthorization` (slack_bolt/middleware/authorization/async_single_team_authorization.py:57), the behavior is consistent across `App` and `AsyncApp`. The `AuthorizeResult` constructor parses the comma-separated header string into a list (`[\"chat:write\", \"commands\"]`).\n\n**Tests** — added `test_success_pattern_with_bot_scopes` for both sync and async middleware, and the mock API server now returns `x-oauth-scopes` headers for bot/user `auth.test` responses.\n\nAll 634 tests pass (adapter tests excluded due to pre-existing missing optional deps). Usage example:\n\n```python\n@app.event(\"app_mention\")\ndef handle(authorize_result):\n    if \"chat:write\" in (authorize_result.bot_scopes or []):\n        ...\n```","time":{"start":1788475435827,"end":1788475441433}}}
